WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.138
The guide lists five dangers.

2
00:00:02.138 --> 00:00:06.912
Counting a G M S A as done for Zero Trust
leaves the Identity pillar at Traditional

3
00:00:06.912 --> 00:00:11.017
for that workload, unseen: score
kept accounts against the scorecard,

4
00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:13.825
and let the island list
carry them to an end date.

5
00:00:13.825 --> 00:00:19.027
Publishing a Kerberos application through
Private Access and forgetting its service

6
00:00:19.027 --> 00:00:21.806
account leaves the back
end outside Zero Trust,

7
00:00:21.806 --> 00:00:23.943
and an S P N move breaks single sign-on:

8
00:00:23.943 --> 00:00:28.219
record the application's service account
and S P N with the published app,

9
00:00:28.219 --> 00:00:31.425
and move the connector's
delegation in step A5's change.

10
00:00:31.425 --> 00:00:36.101
Enforcing the domain controller sensor
on an S P N a service uses blocks

11
00:00:36.101 --> 00:00:38.954
service-to-service
Kerberos: start in audit,

12
00:00:38.954 --> 00:00:42.837
add S P N exclusions to the
exception register, then enforce.

13
00:00:42.837 --> 00:00:48.360
Treating a R H E L keytab like a G M S A
leaves a long-lived key on disk unrotated:

14
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:54.062
inventory keytabs as secrets, and move
the workload to its platform identity.

15
00:00:54.062 --> 00:00:58.655
And one Entra identity for every cloud's
workloads puts secrets or certificates

16
00:00:58.655 --> 00:01:02.974
where the platform would issue temporary
credentials: use native workload

17
00:01:02.974 --> 00:01:07.224
identities, one governance record, and
federation only to cross platforms.
